  "query_preview": "..\nCalm weather early tonight will be gradually replaced by scattered\nshowers and isolated thunderstorms late tonight and lasting through\nThursday night. A large and disorganized tropical disturbance that is\nInvest 95W, is located east-northeast of the Marianas. This system is \nexpected to move through or just north of the Marianas over the next \ntwo days. A col has developed over the Marianas between this \ndisturbance and another system to the northwest. This will keep winds\nfairly light through Wednesday. The unstable airmass and high \nprecipitable water values may allow for heavy showers to develop and \nstall over the islands. This has led to the issuance of a Special \nWeather Statement to highlight the potential flooding risk. High \nuncertainty remains in where the highest rainfall amounts will occur \nand there may be breaks between instances of heavy rainfall. By \nThursday, a monsoonal tail may develop and push north into the \nMarianas, bringing more locally heavy showers and isolated \nthunderstorms, with the highest chances remaining over Guam and Rota.\nSustained winds of 15 to 25 mph will be possible with gusts of 30 \nmph. A brief dry spell is anticipated Friday before another trough \npotentially moves into the region over the weekend.\n\nFor more information, please refer to the Special Weather Statement\nunder WMO header WWMY80 PGUM.",
